{"conversation":"ramat","question":"What is the TACAN for Ramat David?","reply":{"answer":"In DCS: Sinai, the TACAN is VORTAC 84X, 113.7 MHz (RMD). In DCS: Syria, the TACAN is VORTAC 84X, 113.7 MHz (RMD). In Falcon BMS: Israel, the TACAN is VORTAC RMD, channel 84X and 113.7 MHz.","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/#sims-title","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/#sims-title","title":"Ramat David Airbase · Sim maps"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Sinai"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Syria"}]},"ms":2522,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"ramat","question":"What runways are available?","reply":{"answer":"The available runways at Ramat David Airbase are: \n\n* DCS: Syria: 09/27, 11/29, and 33/15 (Source 4).\n* DCS: Sinai: 09/27, 11/29, and 15/33 (Source 3).\n* DCS: Syria (Sim maps): 09/27, 11/29, and 15/33 (Source 2).\n* DCS: Sinai (Sim maps): 09/27, 11/29, and 15/33 (Source 2).","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/#sims-title","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/#sims-title","title":"Ramat David Airbase · Sim maps"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Sinai"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Syria"}]},"ms":2189,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"ramat","question":"Which one runs east-west?","reply":{"answer":"None of the runways listed for Ramat David Airbase run east-west. The available runways and their magnetic headings are: 09/27 (082°/262°), 11/29 (103°/283°), and 15/33 (140°/320°).","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/ramat-david/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-sinai/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Sinai"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-syria/#af-ramat-david","title":"Ramat David · DCS: Syria"}]},"ms":1977,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"ramat","question":"What is the tower freq for Sochi?","reply":{"answer":"In DCS: Caucasus, the tower frequencies for Sochi-Adler are VHF 127.0 MHz, UHF 256.0 MHz, low VHF 39.6 MHz, and HF 4.05 MHz (Source 3). In DCS: Caucasus DCS World, the tower frequencies are 127.0 MHz VHF and 256.0 MHz UHF (Source 2).","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/sochi/#sims-title","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/sochi/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/sochi/#sims-title","title":"Sochi International Airport · Sim maps"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler","title":"Sochi-Adler · DCS: Caucasus"}]},"ms":852,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"ramat","question":"What about its ILS?","reply":{"answer":"In DCS: Caucasus, there is an ILS on runway 06 at 111.1 MHz (ISO) (Source 3).","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/sochi/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler","title":"Sochi-Adler · DCS: Caucasus"}]},"ms":1144,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"concepts","question":"What is angle of attack?","reply":{"answer":"Angle of attack is the angle at which relative wind meets an airfoil, formed by the chord of the airfoil and the direction of the relative wind or between the chord line and the flight path.","ids":["/encyclopedia/terminology/#aoa"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/terminology/#aoa"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#aoa","title":"AOA"}]},"ms":1509,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"concepts","question":"What is a VORTAC?","reply":{"answer":"A VORTAC is a ground navigation aid that gives VOR azimuth, TACAN azimuth, and TACAN distance. It uses paired VHF and UHF bands, and a TACAN-equipped aircraft receives distance from it automatically because the VOR and TACAN channels are paired so that azimuth and distance come from the same station.","ids":["/manual/navigation/air-navigation/#navaids"],"topic":[],"references":[{"id":"/manual/navigation/air-navigation/#navaids","title":"Air Navigation · Radio navigation aids"}]},"ms":1976,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"tomcat-alias","question":"What engines does the Tomcat have?","reply":{"answer":"The F-14 Tomcat's engines depend on the variant: the F-14A uses two Pratt & Whitney TF30-P-414A afterburning turbofans (20,900 lb each), while the F-14B/D uses two General Electric F110-GE-400 (27,000 lb each).","ids":["/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","title":"F-14 Tomcat"}]},"ms":898,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
