{"conversation":"topic-switch","question":"How do flaps affect lift?","reply":{"answer":"High-lift devices, such as flaps, exist to increase CLmax and reduce the stall speed.","ids":["/manual/flight-fundamentals/principles-of-flight/#axes"],"topic":[],"references":[{"id":"/manual/flight-fundamentals/principles-of-flight/#axes","title":"Fixed-Wing Principles of Flight · Axes and flight controls"}]},"ms":837,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"topic-switch","question":"Tell me about radar","reply":{"answer":"Pulse-Doppler radar measures range by timing and closure by Doppler shift, using the shift to reject clutter and track targets in velocity. It can cancel anything that does not move relative to the ground. A target flying across the line of sight falls in the Doppler notch. Pulse repetition frequency (PRF) is the rate a radar sends pulses, which determines how far the radar can measure range unambiguously and how well it measures velocity. Low PRF provides accurate, long, and unambiguous range but makes velocity very hard to track. Medium PRF makes both range and velocity easy to track. High PRF provides excellent velocity resolution but results in ambiguous range. Most modern pulse-Doppler radars include medium and high PRF modes. Radars may also stagger or jitter the interval between pulses to reject range-ambiguous returns and protect against repeater jammers. Additionally, radar correlation involves matching a radar contact to a controller's call by placing the cursor and scan volume at the specified bearing, range, and altitude. The radar horizon is the distance where the earth's curve hides a low target, calculated using an effective earth radius 4/3 of the real one; for example, a radar at 1 mile altitude has a horizon of about 102 miles, and one on a 75 ft mast has a horizon of about 12 miles. A Radar Warning Receiver (RWR) is an electronic warfare support system that detects radar signals, identifies them against an emitter table, and displays them as symbols at a rough relative bearing with tones for new threats, tracks, and missile launches. An RWR provides a threat's type, mode, and rough bearing, but never a fix, and provides nothing for passive infrared missiles.","ids":["/encyclopedia/terminology/#pulse-doppler-radar","/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-horizon","/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-warning-receiver","/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-correlation","/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#recap","/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#prf"],"topic":[],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#pulse-doppler-radar","title":"Pulse-Doppler radar"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-horizon","title":"Radar horizon"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-warning-receiver","title":"Radar warning receiver"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-correlation","title":"Radar correlation"},{"id":"/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#recap","title":"Radar, Warning Receivers and Countermeasures · Key points"},{"id":"/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#prf","title":"Radar, Warning Receivers and Countermeasures · PRF and its trade-offs"}]},"ms":7067,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"comparison","question":"What tower frequencies do Sochi and Kutaisi use?","reply":{"answer":"Sochi-Adler uses VHF 127.0, UHF 256.0, low VHF 39.6, and HF 4.05. Kutaisi uses VHF 134.0, UHF 263.0, low VHF 41.0, and HF 4.4.","ids":["/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler","/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-kutaisi"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/locations/sochi/","/encyclopedia/locations/kutaisi/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-sochi-adler","title":"Sochi-Adler · DCS: Caucasus"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/locations/dcs-caucasus/#af-kutaisi","title":"Kutaisi · DCS: Caucasus"}]},"ms":1839,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
{"conversation":"comparison","question":"Compare the engines of the F-14 and F-16.","reply":{"answer":"The F-14 Tomcat uses two afterburning turbofans, specifically two Pratt & Whitney TF30-P-414A (20,900 lb each) for the F-14A, or two General Electric F110-GE-400 (27,000 lb each) for the F-14B/D. In contrast, the F-16 Fighting Falcon is a single-engine aircraft using either a General Electric F110-GE-129 (29,500 lbf) on the Block 50 or a Pratt & Whitney F100-PW-229 on the Block 52.","ids":["/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/#swing-wings-and-phoenix-missiles","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/"],"topic":["/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/"],"references":[{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/","title":"F-14 Tomcat"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-14/#swing-wings-and-phoenix-missiles","title":"F-14 Tomcat · Swing wings and Phoenix missiles"},{"id":"/encyclopedia/aircraft/f-16/","title":"F-16 Fighting Falcon"}]},"ms":4019,"passed":true,"revision":"16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c"}
